# Markdown

mdsvex reads its own flavour of markdown, [Penguin-Flavoured Markdown](https://github.com/pngwn/MDsveX/blob/main/PFM.md). It is CommonMark with the ambiguous parts taken out, the GFM additions most people use, and a few of its own. Most of a document reads as it does anywhere else. This section covers the rest.

Coming from 0.x, the [migrator](/docs/guides/migrating-from-0x#run-the-migrator) rewrites your documents for you.

::marks

## Different from CommonMark

::::plate[`_text_` and `*text*`](mark="*", tone=strong, id=cm-star)
CommonMark reads `_text_` and `*text*` both as emphasis. mdsvex reads `_text_` as emphasis and `*text*` as strong.

```svx commonmark cue="\*"
_text_ and *text*
```
::::

::::plate[`**text**`, `__text__`](mark="**", tone=strong, id=cm-double)
CommonMark reads them as strong. mdsvex reads strong inside strong, and emphasis inside emphasis.

```svx commonmark cue="\*\*|__"
**text** and __text__
```

:::notes
Write `*text*`.
:::
::::

::::plate[a line of `===` or `---` under a title](mark="===", tone=heading, id=cm-setext)
CommonMark reads a heading. mdsvex reads a paragraph, and `---` is a thematic break.

```svx commonmark cue="^(===|---)$"
Title
===

Title
---
```

:::notes
Write `# Title`.
:::
::::

::::plate[`## Title ##`](mark="##", tone=heading, id=cm-closing)
CommonMark drops the closing `#`. In mdsvex they are text.

```svx commonmark cue="##$"
## Title ##
```
::::

::::plate[four spaces of indent](mark="····", tone=fence, id=cm-indent)
CommonMark reads a code block. In mdsvex it is nothing, indentation means nothing.

```svx commonmark cue="^    "
    code
```

:::notes
Use a fence.
:::
::::

::::plate[`~~~`](mark="~~~", tone=fence, id=cm-tilde)
CommonMark reads a code fence. In mdsvex it is text.

```svx commonmark cue="~~~"
~~~
code
~~~
```

:::notes
A fence is three or more backticks.
:::
::::

::::plate[two spaces at the end of a line](mark="··↵", id=cm-break)
CommonMark reads a line break. In mdsvex they are nothing.

<!-- prettier-ignore -->
```svx commonmark cue=" +$"
one  
two
```

:::notes
End the line with a backslash.
:::
::::

::::plate[a line without `>` after a quoted line](mark=">", tone=quote, id=cm-lazy)
In CommonMark it is still in the quote. In mdsvex the quote has ended.

```svx commonmark cue="^n"
> quoted
not quoted
```
::::

::::plate[`[text]` with a definition somewhere](mark="[a]", tone=link, id=cm-shortcut)
CommonMark reads a link. In mdsvex it is text.

```svx commonmark cue="\[svelte\](?!:)"
[svelte]: https://svelte.dev/

Made with [svelte].
```

:::notes
Write `[text][]`, with the definition above it.
:::
::::

::::plate[`https://example.com`, as GFM links it](mark="https:", tone=link, id=cm-autolink)
GFM reads a link. In mdsvex it is text.

```svx commonmark gfm cue="https://\S+"
See https://example.com
```

:::notes
Write `<https://example.com>`.
:::
::::

::::plate[`{` and `}`](mark="{}", tone=expr, id=cm-brace)
CommonMark reads text. mdsvex reads a Svelte expression.

```svx commonmark cue="[{}]"
One and one is {1 + 1}.
```

:::notes
Write `\{` for a brace.
:::
::::

::::plate[Definitions come first](mark="[]:", tone=link)
A link reference definition comes before the first link that uses it. That lets a document render while it is still arriving, since nothing later in it can change what came before.

```svx result score
[svelte]: https://svelte.dev

Made with [svelte][], see [the site][svelte].
```
::::

::::plate[Inside a word](mark="|_", tone=emph)
A `_` or `*` opens after a space or punctuation and closes before one, so `snake_case_name` and `2*3*4` are text. To mark part of a word, put a `|` on the side that touches a letter. The `|` is not rendered:

```svx result
fan|_tas_|tic
```

:::notes
Curly quotes, dashes and other Unicode punctuation count as punctuation there. Chinese, Japanese and Korean text needs no marker, `这是*重要*的` is strong.
:::
::::

## Added to CommonMark

::::added
:::add[`H~2~O`](mark="~", tone=emph, id=add-sub)
subscript, H~2~O
:::

:::add[`x^2^`](mark="^", tone=emph, id=add-sup)
superscript, x^2^
:::

:::add[`~~gone~~`](mark="~~", tone=emph, id=add-strike)
strikethrough
:::

:::add[`- [x] done`](mark="[x]", tone=item, id=add-task)
a [task list](/docs/learn/markdown#task-lists)
:::

:::add[a pipe table](mark="|", tone=fence, id=add-table)
a [table](/docs/learn/markdown#tables), with header columns and merged cells
:::

:::add[`:name[text]`, `::name`, `:::name`](mark=":", tone=directive, id=add-directive)
a [directive](/docs/guides/directives), your own component
:::

:::add[a `math` fence, `` $`x^2`$ ``](mark="$", tone=code, id=add-math)
[math](/docs/reference/math)
:::

:::add[`[^1]` in the text and `[^1]: a note`](mark="[^]", tone=link, id=add-footnote)
a [footnote](/docs/reference/footnotes)
:::

:::add[`import` lines at the top](mark="import", tone=expr, id=add-import)
[imports](/docs/learn/markdown#svelte-in-markdown) without a `<script>`
:::
::::

## Task lists

::::plate[Task lists](mark="[x]", tone=item, id=tasks)
A list item that starts with `[ ]` or `[x]` and a space is a task:

```svx result
- [x] write the docs
- [ ] ship it
```

:::notes
It works in bulleted and numbered lists at any depth.

A [replaced `li`](/docs/guides/components#element-props) gets `checked` and no checkbox.
:::

:::edges
- The marker needs text after it on its line, so `[x]done` and a bare `- [ ]` are text, and so is a marker anywhere but the start of an item.
- Write `- \[x] text` to start an item with those characters.
:::
::::

## Tables

Tables are GFM pipe tables. A `:` in the delimiter row aligns a column, and `\|` is a bar inside a cell, in a code span too.

::::plate[Row headers](mark="||", tone=fence)
A `||` in the header and delimiter rows makes the columns on one side of it row headers:

```svx result
| option || type | default |
| --- || --- | --- |
| `extensions` || `string[]` | `['.svx']` |
```
::::

::::plate[Merged cells](mark="> ^", tone=fence)
A cell that holds only `>` joins the cell to its left, and one that holds only `^` joins the cell above:

```svx result
| a | > | c |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 2 | 3 |
| ^ | 4 | 5 |
```

:::edges
- Write `\>` or `\^` for a cell of that one character.
- A row is one line, and a table ends at a blank line or at any line that would end a paragraph, such as a heading.
:::
::::

## Svelte in markdown

::::plate[Markdown inside Svelte](mark="{#}", tone=logic)
Components, elements, expressions and blocks go anywhere, and markdown works inside them with no blank lines around it:

```svx result score
<Box>
# A title
</Box>

{#if published}
Some *strong* text.
{/if}
```

:::notes
A block quote, a list item, an element, a Svelte block and a container directive all hold the same blocks the document does, so a table or a `{#if}` is the same thing inside a list item as at the top.
:::
::::

::::plate[A paragraph of only tags gets no `<p>`.](mark="<C/>", tone=comp)
`<Chart />` on a line of its own is the component and nothing else, and `<Badge /> new today` is a paragraph.

```svx result
<Chart />

<Badge /> new today
```
::::

::::plate[A block level element that starts a line is a block.](mark="<div>", tone=comp)
`<div>one</div> two` is the `<div>` and then a paragraph, `two`.

```svx result
<div>one</div> two
```
::::

::::plate[`<script>`, `<style>`, `<pre>` and `<textarea>` hold text.](mark="<pre>", tone=comp)
Nothing inside them is markdown.
::::

::::plate[Imports can stand at the top](mark="import", tone=expr)
They stand after any frontmatter, without a `<script>`, and go into the document's script:

```svx result
import Chart from './Chart.svelte'

<Chart />
```
::::

::::plate[A brace is an expression.](mark="{}", tone=expr)
For a brace as text, write `\{`, use `&#123;`, or put it in code, where a brace is always text.

```svx result
One and one is {1 + 1}, not \{1 + 1}.
```
::::
